      Hot Visual Computing Software, Blazing Alpha Performance
         Headline DIGITAL Semiconductor Exhibit at NAB Show


      ...New Alpha 21164-600MHz, 21164PC Chips Star in Demos...


LAS VEGAS, April 7, 1997 -- The world's fastest PCs and workstations 
are showing off the hottest Windows NT software packages for 3-D 
animation, visual effects, and film and video editing in the Digital 
Semiconductor booth at the National Association of Broadcasters 
(NAB) show.

     At Booth S3308 in the Sands Expo Center, Digital Semiconductor, 
a Digital Equipment Corporation business, is showcasing the 
industry-leading performance of its Alpha microprocessors.  More 
than 15 system and software vendors who supply products to leading 
film and video studios are appearing in the "Alpha Pavilion" to 
demonstrate visual computing with performance and quality unmatched 
elsewhere in the industry.  Digital Semiconductor is also 
demonstrating MPEG video solutions based on its 21230 video codec 
chip.

New Software for Alpha

     Among new products being shown at the Digital Semiconductor 
booth are form.Z RenderZone v.2.9 3-D modeling software with organic modeling
enhancements from auto.des.sys; Denim Software's Illuminaire Composition image
editing suite; Metatools' Bryce 2 3-D landscape generator; a new release of
the Lightwave3D animation product from NewTek, Inc.; and Houdini, a new 3-D
rendering package from Side Effects Software.

     The Alpha Pavilion also features prototype systems using 
Digital's recently announced, record-setting Alpha 21164-600MHz 
microprocessor as well as the low-cost Alpha 21164PC chip.  The 
Alpha 21164-600MHz system takes on a dual-processor Pentium Pro 
system in a side-by-side demonstration of NewTek's Lightwave3D V5.0 
3-D modeling/rendering software.

'Enviable Reputation'

     "System and software vendors who use the Alpha NT platform have 
built an enviable reputation for Alpha in the broadcast industry for 
outstanding 3-D video imaging performance, quality, and 
productivity," said Tim Miller, Digital Semiconductor's strategic 
marketing manager for Alpha microprocessors.  "Alpha systems and 
Windows NT applications enable artists to push their creative 
envelopes with superior floating point performance and lightning 
system response."

     Alpha-optimized software being demonstrated in the Digital 
Semiconductor booth includes Digital Fusion for video effects from EyeOn
Software; in:sync's RazorPro editing software; MeshPaint3D texture and
painting software from Positron Publishing; SOFTIMAGE 3D modeling/rendering
software from Softimage, Inc.; Sonic Foundry's Sound Forge digital sound
studio; and Star Media's Video Action NT video editor. Other applications
running on Alpha platforms include Equilibrium's Debabelizer digital
composition software and Fractal Design's Paint (2D) and Detailer (3D) imaging
software.

     System vendors demonstrating Alpha PCs and workstations in 
Booth S3308 include Bushey Virtual Construction, Carrera Computers, 
DeskStation Technology, Digital Equipment Corporation, Enorex 
Microsystems, The Panda Project, and Polywell Computers.

MPEG Video Editing Demos

     Digital Semiconductor is also cooperating with Innovacom Inc., 
and VITEC Multimedia to show MPEG-1 solutions using the 21230 video 
codec.  Innovacom is showing its 21230-based MPEG-1 capture and 
authoring solution as well as its own MPEG-2 encoding and DVD 
authoring tools.  VITEC Multimedia is showing the MPEG-Cut Machine, 
a complete MPEG-1 editing solution integrating the VITEC RT5 PCI 
realtime encoder, VIDEO Clip MPEG and VIDEO CD authoring software.

     Digital Semiconductor, a Digital Equipment Corporation business 
headquartered in Hudson, Massachusetts, designs, manufactures and 
markets industry-leading semiconductor products including Alpha 
microprocessors and PCI chips for networking, bridging, and 
multimedia, plus low-power StrongARM microprocessors under license 
from Advanced RISC Machines Ltd.  Mitsubishi Electric Corporation 
and Samsung Electronics Company Ltd. are alternate sources for Alpha 
microprocessors.  Web site:  http://www.digital.com/semiconductor

